# 1. Is it a good idea?

Yes — plainly, and not reluctantly. It is close to the only shape the
foundations permit: any lease built as mechanism (a timer that fires, a
registry of live leases, a synchronous `renew` call, a background renewal
manager) violates at least three of the six invariants by inspection —
implicit control flow, callbacks, hidden global state — and Jini's lease
violates all three while also shipping an absolute clock. The proposal keeps
Jini's actual insight (reclaim without detecting death, without consensus,
without anyone registering) and discards Jini's engineering. That said, two
of the task's probes find real work the proposal must do, and I do it below
rather than wave at it.

**"Expiry is interpretation" versus a socket closed at a particular moment.**
This survives, because the moment was never going to be anyone's but the
grantor's. Only the grantor can close its socket, so "when does the socket
close" was always answered by the grantor's clock and the grantor's cadence —
Jini merely *disguised* this with an absolute expiration both sides pretended
to share. Under the proposal: lapse is a conclusion (interpretation), reclaim
is an act (execution), and the invariant that interpretation and execution
must not collapse into one layer is precisely the rule that keeps them apart.
Nothing fires on lapse; the grantor's judge concludes lapse, and the grantor's
driver reclaims at the next step of a cadence it already owns — the serving
composition stepping its forwarders, the liveness interpreter between pings.
Reclaim latency is therefore bounded by judgment cadence, and that bound is
honest and declarable, which is all a socket ever needed. The corollary must
be owned: lapse has no instant, so holder and grantor can disagree, and the
disagreement is resolved not by protocol but by margin — the holder renews
well inside the tolerance. Axiom 2 has teeth here; the design turns the teeth
into a tuning parameter.

**Conflict with the axioms and invariants.** None conflict; two are
constitutive. Axiom 1: grant and renewal are appends. Axiom 2: "expired" is a
perspective on facts taken with a clock — this is the axiom applied to time,
not a strain on it. Axiom 3: the coordination state of a lease is queryable
data, which Jini's live object forbids by construction. The invariants do the
demolition work on the alternative: no callbacks (a renewal is an append), no
implicit control flow (nothing fires on lapse), no hidden global state (no
lease registry — the record is the medium), no shared mutable state (the
record accretes rather than mutates).

**Genuine generality or speculative?** Not speculative, and the evidence is
already in the tree. Two consumers are agreed in the implementation plan
(pause-as-lease, liveness); `dao.space.v0.md` *already designed this shape*
for work claims — its rejection of JavaSpace leasing rejected auto-expiry
destroying tuples and a renewal state machine, and its fix was
claim datoms plus a watchdog comparing `:work/claimed-at` to its own clock,
which is leases-as-datoms in everything but the name; `dao.space.schema.md`
defers cross-stream claim races to "leases, tie-breaks" in an unwritten
`dao.space.stigmergy.md`; and `dao.space.schema.datomic.md`'s open question —
how the single-writer capability is durably leased and recovered — is another.
But the generality is a *consequence*, not a justification, and it stays
honest only if the document forbids the library: no `RenewalManager`, no
lease daemon, no shared judging machinery beyond functions a composition
hands to its interpreters. The moment someone ships a manager thread, Jini is
back.

**Clock discipline with no absolute time on the wire.** This is the design's
hardest content and it has a clean answer: durations travel, instants never
do; the judge dates arrivals with its own clock; a timestamp inside a fact is
its author's private observation, provenance only. The datom's `t` cannot do
the job — it is stream-local, a gauge, exactly as `datom.md` says — so the
judge reads the only time it can trust (its own) against the only order it
can trust (positions on its own medium). Jini's `getExpiration()` in the
holder's milliseconds is the bug this deletes: clock skew between holder and
grantor made leases expire early or late and neither side could tell which.
Here the holder never holds an expiry to be wrong about; it holds a cadence
and a tolerance, and surprise reclaim — read off its own outcomes,
`:dao.stream/closed`, a deposited lifecycle event — is information it adjusts
to.

**Renewal facts evicted by bounded retention.** The trap is judging absence
from behind a gap: an evicted renewal is indistinguishable from a withheld
one, and a grantor that reclaims on the strength of an eviction it caused
itself has made its own retention policy an adversary of its own leases. The
rule that fixes it also fixes judge restart and fresh attachment in one
stroke: **absence is evidence only under coverage** — the judge drains its
medium to `:dao.stream/blocked` and has observed for at least one tolerance
window before it may conclude lapse. Under that rule a gap means *unknown*
for one cycle; the recovery cursor is at the newest position, so a holder
that keeps renewing is seen immediately, and a holder exploiting
gap-forbearance must nonetheless send the renewal it was asked for. The lease
record is a window, not an archive, and that suffices, because judgment is
always about now.

**The hostile holder.** It gains nothing the grantor does not already
control, because of one inversion the vocabulary must state and Jini's does
not: renewal is petition, not contract. A lease never binds the grantor; the
grantor's caps (max duration per grant, max total held time, max renewals)
are its interpreter's configuration, judged silently, published in the grant
or not at all. A renewal flood is not a lease problem but ordinary volume on
an ordinary medium, answered by admission and retention policy. And in the
specific DaoStream case the plan already recorded the structural answer: a
client pausing forever harms only itself, because the served stream evicts on
its own schedule regardless of who is attached.

One honest correction to the proposal's own framing: "needs no new mechanism
— only a vocabulary" is ninety percent true and must not be rounded up. The
vocabulary includes the correlation rule, the clock rules, and the judging
discipline above — that *is* the design content — and judges with clocks and
cadences must exist. But they already exist (the serving driver, the liveness
pinger); nothing new must be built beneath the interpreters. The claim
survives with its edges shown.

# 2. Proposed `docs/design/dao.lease.md`

```markdown
# Dao Lease Design

Status: proposed design target, derived from and subordinate to
[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). This document is a sibling of
[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md): it defines a vocabulary of facts, not a
transport, and it names none of DaoStream's operations as its own. It is the
durable home the WebSocket specification's deferred pause vocabulary asked
for (`dao.stream.ws.md`, Deferred — "a pause vocabulary both ends must
recognize is interoperable wire content, and it requires a durable home
before it is built"); that specification owns how the words travel, this one
owns what they mean.

## Purpose

Four problems share one shape: how to stop caring about something that may
never answer. A pause request whose requester dies; a connection that is
dead while both endpoints still believe it live; a departed client whose
server-side resources no one will come to release; work claimed by a worker
that will never finish it. In each, one party holds something — a resource,
a restraint, a deferral — on behalf of another party that may go silent
without saying goodbye.

Java Jini answered this class with the lease: a grant that lapses unless
renewed, so a dead holder's resources are reclaimed without anyone detecting
death and without consensus. The insight is kept whole here. The design is
not. A Jini lease is an object with methods held by the grantee; `renew` is
a synchronous remote call that blocks and fails by exception;
`getExpiration()` returns absolute time in the holder's milliseconds; a
`LeaseRenewalManager` renews from a background thread. Every one of those is
a thing this project forbids: a live object that cannot cross a boundary, a
blocking call, an exception where data is owed, a clock no two hosts share,
and a scheduler nobody composed. What survives the translation is the part
Jini got right: **reclamation by absence of renewal** — the holder leaves a
trace saying *I still want this, as of now*, and the grantor reclaims when
it encounters the absence of a recent trace. Nobody registers, nobody is
notified, nobody detects death.

Two axioms bear on this document, named once so the sections below can cite
rather than restate them:

1. **Everything is a Stream** — a lease is granted and renewed by appending
   facts, and judged by reading them.
2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — nothing about a lease is true
   until an interpreter concludes it; lapse is a perspective taken on facts
   with a clock.

One invariant does the load-bearing separation: **interpretation and
execution must not collapse into the same layer.** Concluding that a lease
has lapsed is interpretation; reclaiming the resource is execution,
performed by the grantor's own code at its own cadence. The remaining
denials follow the same way: a renewal is an append, never a callback; the
record of leases is the medium, never a registry; lapse has no instant, so
nothing fires.

## What a Lease Is

A lease is the accreted trace of a relation between two parties, expressed
as facts on a medium. No lease object exists. There is no handle to hold, no
token to present, no method to call; the holder of a lease holds nothing but
its own knowledge of the correlation value and its own renewal cadence.
Dropping that knowledge is how a holder detaches — there is nothing to
unregister.

The parties are named for what they do:

- The **grantor** is the party that defers an action it is otherwise
  entitled to take — reclaiming a resource, resuming a send, dropping a
  connection — and that judges lapse. Its clock is the only clock in the
  relation that matters.
- The **holder** is the party that benefits from the deferral and sustains
  it by leaving traces. In the resource case the holder holds the resource;
  in the pause case the holder holds the pause; in both, what it actually
  holds is the grantor's continuing deference.

The vocabulary is four facts:

| Fact       | Author    | Means                                                        |
|------------|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
| `grant`    | grantor   | A deferral exists, on these terms, for this tolerance.       |
| `renew`    | holder    | Still wanted, as of now; may carry a requested duration.     |
| `release`  | holder    | Done early; the grantor may act.                             |
| `revoke`   | grantor   | Reclaimed early; the grantor has already acted.              |

Three rules govern them. **A renew no grant answers is a request for one.**
The grantor may mint a lease on receiving it, or ignore it; the first
pheromone deposit is not a special kind of deposit. **Renewal is petition,
not contract.** A lease never binds the grantor, and the holder is never
bound to renew; whether a renewal extends the deferral, and by how much, is
the grantor's judgment under its own policy — maximum granted duration,
maximum total deferral, maximum renewals — which is configuration supplied
to its interpreter, never a wire negotiation and never a promise. Where the
grantor caps silently, the holder's evidence is the resumption itself.
**Release and revoke are records, not prerequisites.** The actions they name
are taken without appending anything; the facts exist so that observers can
read what happened. Within one lease, the latest fact on the judging medium
wins — a `release` followed by a `renew` is a contradictory trace, and the
judge reads the newest word.

All facts of one lease are correlated by a shared value on the medium where
they are judged; what mints that value is the composition. A WebSocket
boundary already mints one per attachment, and its deposit envelopes already
carry it, so lease facts riding a request medium are groupable for free. A
duration is a plain number; its unit is declared by the binding that carries
it. Exact key spellings, and the mapping to `[e a v t m]` where the medium
is `dao.space`, are settled by each consumer at its own wire or schema gate
— the settled content here is the four kinds, their authors, and their
meanings.

## Clocks

No instant crosses a boundary; durations may. Everything else follows:

- The judge dates arrivals with its own clock at observation. A timestamp
  inside a fact is its author's private observation — provenance for an
  auditor, never authority for a judge.
- The datom's `t` is not time. It is stream-local and monotonic, a gauge;
  across streams it orders nothing. The only order a judge may use is the
  order on its own medium, which is per-lease and sufficient, because no
  lease is ever judged against another.
- The grant publishes the tolerance the judge will apply, so a holder can
  choose a cadence; a renew may carry a requested duration the judge is free
  to cap.
- Skew is absorbed by margin, not by protocol. A holder renews at a cadence
  strictly inside the tolerance — renewing at a third of it gives three
  chances to be heard — and assumes the judge's clock may read later than
  its own.
- Surprise reclaim is data. The holder that believed its lease live reads
  its own outcomes — `:dao.stream/closed`, a deposited lifecycle event —
  adjusts its cadence or surrenders, and is correct either way, because a
  lease is an optimization, never a correctness dependency: every consumer
  must remain correct if reclaimed at any instant.

Two parties with different clocks and different tolerances may disagree
about whether a lease has lapsed, and both be right in their frames. That is
Axiom 2 with teeth, and the margin is the protocol's way of making the
disagreement survivable rather than impossible.

## Judging Lapse

Nothing fires when a lease lapses. An interpreter concludes it, and the
conclusion is only as good as its coverage of the medium. The judging
discipline is normative:

1. **Drain before judging.** Read the medium from your cursor to
   `:dao.stream/blocked`. Absence is a statement about the medium's current
   state, not about its evicted history.
2. **A gap is unknown, not absence.** On `:dao.stream/gap`, recover the
   cursor and judge nothing lapsed this cycle; the recovery cursor is at the
   newest position, so a renewing holder is seen immediately. A judge that
   reclaims on the strength of an eviction it caused itself has made its own
   retention policy an adversary of its own leases.
3. **Bootstrap by coverage, not by faith.** A judge that has not observed
   the medium for at least the tolerance it granted — after a restart, after
   a fresh attachment — may not conclude lapse. The same rule that governs
   gaps governs unread intervals.
4. **Take the latest fact.** Within the lease, the newest attributable fact
   decides: a `release` ends it, a `renew` dates it, a `revoke` is already
   over.
5. **Compare and conclude.** Your now, minus your arrival-dating of the
   latest `renew`, against the tolerance you granted or capped.

Judging is not acting. The reclaim — closing the socket, resuming the
forwarder, returning the claim to the pool — is execution by the grantor's
driver, on the cadence it already owns, and its latency is bounded by that
cadence. A tolerance smaller than the judgment cadence is a composition
defect: the judge cannot honor a window it cannot see the end of.

A judge's own table of leases — correlation value to last-observed renewal —
is interpreter state, owned as a cursor is owned, lost as a cursor is lost,
and governed by the bootstrap rule when it is lost.

## The Same Words, Inside Out

The pause reading is where the vocabulary earns its generality, because it
inverts every Jini instinct and still reads naturally. A reader falling
behind asks its server to stop sending for a while. The server is the
grantor — it defers an action it is entitled to take, sending, and it judges
lapse; the reader is the holder — it holds the pause and sustains it by
renewing while it is still behind. A reader that dies mid-pause is
indistinguishable from one that caught up, and the system returns to sending
on its own. If the vocabulary only fit the resource case it would be Jini
with datoms; the pause case is proof it is the relation, not the resource,
that was named.

## What a Lease May Never Do

- **No lease object.** No handle, no protocol, no methods; no `renew` that
  waits or throws; no `getExpiration`, because no absolute time exists to
  return. A lease is not a value you hold; it is a trace you leave.
- **No renewal manager.** Nothing schedules renewals on the holder's behalf
  as hidden machinery. A holder's renewal loop is an interpreter its runtime
  drives, or it is nothing.
- **No registry of leases.** Nothing maps lease identities to holders or
  expirations. The record of leases is the medium and nothing else.
- **No `expired?` predicate.** A predicate answer is stale the moment it
  returns; the judgment is the interpreter's, made under coverage, for its
  own reasons.
- **Nothing fires on lapse.** No expiry event, no watcher, no push. Lapse
  has no instant and makes no announcement.
- **A lease is not a capability.** A capability is shown — presented as
  proof; a lease is kept up — repeated as petition. Whether a lapsed lease
  ends access is the resource owner's policy; authentication of any kind is
  not this vocabulary's to give, and a medium where attribution matters
  needs its own story for it.
- **A lease never joins DaoStream.** No lease operation appears among the
  seven; no lease fact is judged at a deposit; a boundary that carries
  renewals wraps them like any other payload and looks inside them for
  nothing.

## Retention and the Medium

Lease facts live on whatever medium the composition already uses — the
boundary's deposit medium, or `dao.space` — under that medium's ordinary
retention. There is no lease-specific medium, because a second medium with
its own retention and its own interpreters is the thing ADR 0003 forbids.
Bounded retention is not a hazard but the natural shape: the lease record is
a window, not an archive, and judgment is always about now. The gap rule in
Judging Lapse is the whole of the interaction between leases and eviction.

A lapsed lease destroys no data. What a lapsed lease ends is the grantor's
deference, nothing else — which is the precise line between this and the
JavaSpace mistake the project recorded long before this document: tuples
that auto-expired, and work lost to a lease that lapsed mid-processing.
Here the trace remains as long as retention allows, a holder that keeps
working after lapse merely produces facts a judge may weigh differently, and
the vocabulary itself destroys nothing.

## Out of Scope

- Clock synchronization, in any form. The design assumes skew and absorbs
  it with margin.
- Consensus about lapse, or any multi-grantor agreement. One resource, one
  judge.
- Tie-breaks and fairness among competing petitioners for one resource —
  claim races belong to the coordination layer that consumes this
  vocabulary (`dao.space.stigmergy.md`, where three documents already point).
- Priority and preemption policy between leases.
- Retransmission, ordering, or delivery guarantees for renewal facts —
  carriage belongs to the stream, and a lost renewal is a lapse the margin
  exists to absorb.
- Attribution and authentication of holders.
- Liveness probe mechanics — whether a solicited probe is a protocol frame
  or an ordinary value, at what interval, and what answers it — which
  belongs to the transport that carries the lease.

## Consumers

Agreed now: the WebSocket slice's flow-control pause and connection
liveness (`dao.stream.implementation-plan.md`, 4d), and the dead-client
reclamation it names. Recorded for the future: work-claim leases in
`dao.space`, whose watchdog shape this vocabulary names; the single-writer
capability lease of `dao.space.schema.datomic.md`'s open questions; claim
races deferred to `dao.space.stigmergy.md`. A consumer is added by citing
this vocabulary and binding its key spellings at its own gate — never by
extending it with mechanism.
```

# 3. What it does not replace

- **Pause requests — solved.** The stuck-pause failure mode (lost resume,
  dead reader, sender stopped forever) is eliminated by construction:
  absence of renewal *is* the resume. What remains bounded is duration —
  the grantor's caps decide how long one reader can hold a pause — which is
  policy, not failure.
- **Connection liveness — bounded, not solved.** The lease bounds how long
  a dead connection persists (tolerance = interval × unanswered probes) but
  does not detect death, and cannot shorten the window below the judge's
  cadence. A live-but-silent peer still needs a solicited probe, and
  whether that probe is a ping frame or a value is carriage, not lease.
- **A dead client holding server-side resources — bounded, not solved.**
  Reclamation no longer waits on detection and needs no consensus, but it
  is never instant (judgment cadence) and it does not return work the dead
  client already consumed — that is a work-claim lease at the `dao.space`
  layer, a consumer, not a consequence.
- **A connection cancelled before it resolves — untouched, and rightly.**
  The team's verdict is correct, and the reason is sharper than "wrong
  tool": a lease is for a holder that may be *silently* gone, and
  cancellation is an *explicit* withdrawal of interest by a live party. The
  mechanism is already ordinary control flow — `close!` the handle, drop
  the continuation, and let the late-arriving resolution event land on the
  medium as correlatable data nobody is obliged to read. A lease would be
  worse than useless here: it would keep the pending connection alive
  until lapse instead of closing it at the earliest possible moment, which
  is the opposite of what cancellation wants. The only lease-relevant
  residue of problem four is its remote half — a half-open connection the
  server holds for a client that will never come back — and that is
  problem three.

# 4. The seam to DaoStream

The proposed split is confirmed, with its edges drawn precisely:

- **`dao.lease.md` owns meaning; `dao.stream.ws.md` owns carriage, and
  neither reaches across.** The lease document names no frame, no close
  code, and no event kind. The WebSocket document defines how the four
  facts travel and how reclaim is signalled.
- **Renewals are ordinary payload values, not a new frame type.** This is
  settled by principle, not preference: a boundary judges nothing, and a
  renewal is a value; the pause note in `dao.stream.ws.md` already carries
  it ("pause requests travel as ordinary values on the socket the client
  already writes to"). On the server they arrive on the request medium per
  *Serving*, enveloped with `:ws/attachment`, so lease correlation is free.
  The wire encoding of the facts settles at the plan's wire-contract gate,
  alongside the descriptor keys — the same anti-pressure rule that gate
  already exists to enforce.
- **Reclaim needs its own application-range close code, distinct from the
  ended-stream code.** `dao.stream.ws.md`'s three outcomes — frame arrived /
  nothing arrived / unreachable — gain a second deliberate-close sub-case:
  ended means *stop, the stream is gone*; reclaimed means *deliberately
  closed, the stream survives, reattach is permitted* — renew faster or go
  away. The client's response differs (stop vs reattach-with-adjustment),
  which is the whole reason the codes must differ. The adapter deposits a
  lifecycle event distinct from `:ws/closed` and `:ws/ended` (`:ws/reclaimed`
  is the natural name; the name is `dao.stream.ws.md`'s to own). The
  reclaiming close is performed by the serving composition, exactly as the
  ended-stream close is — the judge concludes, the composition acts.
- **Liveness carriage stays where it already sits**: the Deferred item on
  ping/pong versus deposited values, intervals, and unanswered-probe counts
  remains `dao.stream.ws.md`'s, since a solicited probe is the grantor
  petitioning for evidence and its mechanics are wire.
- **The plan's 4d section is the first binding, not a second specification.**
  Its pause-as-lease, liveness, caps, and harms-only-itself sections become
  the consumer record of this vocabulary; nothing in them changes, and the
  words they use get their durable home.
